  "slug": "evolution-sexual-efec",
  "summary": "Five new preprints use genome-scale data to tie phenotypic and lineage divergence to geography, introgression and incomplete lineage sorting in birds, insects, mammals and plants.",
  "synthesis": "The preprints test how phenotypic and lineage divergence arise using whole-genome or multilocus data across owls, butterflies, deer, bromeliads and water voles. Tasmanian masked owls combine larger, darker phenotypes with genomic and niche divergence from mainland birds. Heliconius cydno diversification is linked to Andean geography, and ivory:mir-193 is identified for hindwing band variation. In Old World deer, incomplete lineage sorting dominated Miocene divergences whereas gene flow shaped Pliocene-Pleistocene splits. Puya's Septentrional Clade is proposed as an adaptive radiation tied to paramo expansion. Water voles show divergence near 400 ka and mito-nuclear discordance. What is new is the pairing of phenotypic or ecological patterns with explicit genomic evidence of isolation, introgression and sorting, rather than phenotype-only inference.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
